-- Q1: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred numerator add-back for diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverton Corp. reports net income of $750,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding. The company has 5,000 shares of $100 par, 7% convertible preferred stock, each share convertible into 3 common shares. Preferred dividends of $35,000 were declared. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.75", "$3.58", "$3.49", "$3.33"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method requires adding back the $35,000 preferred dividend to the numerator ($750,000) and adding 15,000 potential shares (5,000 × 3) to the denominator (215,000). Diluted EPS is $750,000 / 215,000 = $3.49. Choice D ($3.33) incorrectly fails to add back preferred dividends, double-counting the deduction while still adding conversion shares to the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Effect of mid-year share repurchase on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenwood Industries reports net income of $480,000 with 150,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On September 1, Glenwood repurchases 30,000 shares. There are no preferred dividends. What is the effect of the repurchase on basic EPS compared to if no repurchase had occurred?', '["EPS decreases because fewer shares generate lower net income", "EPS increases from $3.20 to $3.43 because the weighted-average shares decrease from 150,000 to 140,000", "EPS is unchanged because a share repurchase is a financing activity with no effect on the EPS denominator", "EPS increases from $3.20 to $4.00 because the 30,000 repurchased shares are removed for the full year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, repurchased shares reduce the weighted-average denominator from the repurchase date. The weighted average drops to 140,000 (150,000 × 8/12 + 120,000 × 4/12), increasing EPS from $3.20 to $3.43 because the same net income is spread over fewer weighted shares. Choice D ($4.00) incorrectly removes shares for the entire year rather than time-weighting from September 1.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Shares issued in business combination weighted-average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Corp. has 100,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, Pinehurst issues 40,000 shares to acquire Lakeside Inc. in a business combination. Net income for the year is $630,000 with no preferred stock. What is basic EPS?', '["$6.30", "$5.25", "$4.50", "$4.85"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. Pinehurst''s weighted average is 130,000 shares (100,000 × 3/12 + 140,000 × 9/12), yielding basic EPS of $4.85 ($630,000 / 130,000). Choice C ($4.50) incorrectly treats the 40,000 acquisition shares as outstanding for the full year, overstating the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L3 — Loss from continuing operations as EPS control number
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarwood Holdings reports a net loss from continuing operations of $320,000 and income from discontinued operations of $180,000, resulting in a net loss of $140,000. The company has 80,000 weighted-average common shares and 10,000 in-the-money stock options that would add 3,000 incremental shares. Under ASC 260, how should the stock options be treated when computing diluted EPS?', '["The options are excluded from diluted EPS because the control number — loss from continuing operations — is negative, making all potentially dilutive securities antidilutive", "The options are included in diluted EPS for the discontinued operations line only, since that line shows income", "The options are included for all line items because the net loss is partially offset by discontinued operations income", "The options are included for all line items because they are in-the-money and therefore always dilutive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19A, the control number for determining dilution is income or loss from continuing operations. Because Briarwood reports a $320,000 loss from continuing operations, including incremental shares would reduce the loss per share, which is antidilutive. All potentially dilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS for every line item. Choice D is incorrect because in-the-money status is irrelevant when the control number is a loss.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Definition of complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what defines a complex capital structure?', '["A capital structure that includes more than one class of common stock", "A capital structure that includes securities with the potential to dilute EPS", "A capital structure with total debt exceeding total equity", "A capital structure that includes both domestic and foreign subsidiaries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, such as convertible bonds, stock options, warrants, or convertible preferred stock. Choice A is incorrect because having multiple classes of common stock alone does not create a complex capital structure unless those securities have dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haverford Inc. reports net income of $900,000 with 200,000 common shares and 50,000 shares of Series A preferred stock. The preferred stock participates equally with common stock in dividends above its stated rate of $2.00 per share. Total declared dividends are $600,000. After the preferred holders receive $100,000 ($2.00 × 50,000), the remaining $500,000 is shared proportionally among all shareholders. Evaluate whether the two-class method applies and determine basic EPS for common shareholders.', '["$4.00", "$4.50", "$3.20", "$1.50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method is required for participating securities. Distributed dividends to common: $500,000 × 200/250 = $400,000. Undistributed earnings ($300,000) allocated to common: $300,000 × 200/250 = $240,000. Common EPS = ($400,000 + $240,000) / 200,000 = $3.20. Choice A ($4.00) incorrectly deducts only the $100,000 stated preferred dividend without allocating the participation rights on remaining dividends and undistributed earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average common shares outstanding
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the term weighted-average common shares outstanding mean for EPS purposes?', '["The number of shares reflecting the time-weighted portion of shares outstanding during the reporting period", "The total shares authorized by the corporate charter as of year-end", "The average of beginning and ending shares outstanding divided by two", "The number of shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding is calculated by time-weighting each change in shares during the period from the date the change occurs. This reflects the proportion of the period each share was outstanding. Choice C is incorrect because a simple average of beginning and ending shares does not accurately reflect mid-period changes in shares outstanding.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Definition of potential common stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is potential common stock?', '["Stock that has been authorized but not yet issued by the board of directors", "Stock held in treasury that may be reissued in the future", "Common stock owned by a subsidiary of the reporting entity", "A security or contract that may entitle its holder to obtain common stock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, potential common stock is defined as a security or other contract that may entitle its holder to obtain common stock during the reporting period or after the end of the reporting period. Examples include convertible bonds, stock options, and warrants. Choice A is incorrect because authorized but unissued shares do not represent contracts or securities that could result in dilution.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements in conformity with GAAP", "Only entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market", "All entities with total revenues exceeding $10 million", "Only entities that have both common and preferred stock outstanding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities whose common stock or potential common stock is publicly traded or that are filing with a regulatory agency for public issuance. Private entities are exempt from EPS disclosure. Choice A is incorrect because nonpublic entities preparing GAAP financial statements are not required to present EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what characterizes an antidilutive security?', '["A security that has been retired during the reporting period", "A security that was issued below par value", "A security whose inclusion in diluted EPS would increase EPS or decrease loss per share", "A security whose exercise price exceeds par value of the common stock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, an antidilutive security is one whose inclusion in the diluted EPS computation would increase EPS or decrease loss per share. Antidilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS because their inclusion would make results appear more favorable to common shareholders. Choice D is incorrect because antidilutive status depends on the effect on EPS, not on the relationship between exercise price and par value.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Stock split after reporting period but before issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mercer Industries has 150,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $600,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. On February 10, 2026, before the financial statements are issued, Mercer declares a 2-for-1 stock split. How should this event affect the EPS reported in the December 31, 2025 financial statements?', '["The weighted-average shares must be retroactively adjusted to 300,000, and basic EPS is reported as $2.00", "The stock split is disclosed in the notes but does not change the reported EPS of $4.00", "The weighted-average shares remain at 150,000 with basic EPS of $4.00 and a pro forma disclosure of $2.00", "The stock split is recorded as a subsequent event requiring adjustment only in the following year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the reporting period but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively reflected in the weighted-average shares for all periods presented. The 2-for-1 split doubles shares to 300,000, producing EPS of $2.00. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive adjustment of the denominator, not merely footnote disclosure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Tax effect error on convertible bond interest add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kessler Corp. reports net income of $400,000 with 100,000 weighted-average shares. Kessler has $500,000 of 8% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond convertible into 20 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. When computing diluted EPS, the controller adds the full $40,000 pretax interest to the numerator. What is the effect of this approach?', '["Diluted EPS is correctly stated at $4.00 because convertible bond interest is always added at the pretax amount", "Diluted EPS is understated because additional conversion shares should not be added when bonds are outstanding", "Diluted EPS is overstated; the controller should not add back any interest because the bonds are convertible", "Diluted EPS is overstated because the interest add-back should be the after-tax amount of $30,000, yielding correct diluted EPS of $3.91"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method requires adding back after-tax interest expense, not pretax. The correct add-back is $30,000 ($40,000 × 0.75), yielding diluted EPS of $3.91 ($430,000 / 110,000). The controller''s pretax add-back overstates the numerator by $10,000 and inflates diluted EPS to $4.00. Choice A is incorrect because interest on convertible bonds must always be tax-effected before inclusion in the diluted EPS numerator.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which method is used to calculate the dilutive effect of stock options on earnings per share?', '["The if-converted method", "The treasury stock method", "The weighted-average method", "The effective interest method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method is used to compute the dilutive effect of stock options and warrants on diluted EPS. Under this method, assumed exercise proceeds are used to repurchase shares at the average market price. Choice A is incorrect because the if-converted method applies to convertible securities such as bonds and preferred stock, not to options.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Anti-dilution sequencing with multiple securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dunmore Corp. reports net income of $1,200,000 with 300,000 weighted-average shares. Three potentially dilutive securities exist: (1) 20,000 stock options adding 5,000 incremental shares with no numerator effect, (2) $400,000 of 5% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) convertible into 12,000 shares, and (3) 2,000 shares of $100 par, 12% convertible preferred stock convertible into 3,000 common shares. Evaluate the proper sequencing and determine which securities to include in diluted EPS.', '["Include options first, then bonds; exclude preferred because its $8.00 per-share impact exceeds the cumulative diluted EPS — diluted EPS is $3.76", "Include all three securities in order of per-share impact — diluted EPS is $3.80", "Include bonds first because they have the largest numerator adjustment, then options, then preferred — diluted EPS is $3.63", "Exclude all potentially dilutive securities because the preferred stock is antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, potentially dilutive securities must be ranked from most dilutive to least dilutive. Options ($0 / 5,000 = $0.00) are included first, then bonds ($15,000 / 12,000 = $1.25). The preferred stock''s per-share impact of $8.00 ($24,000 / 3,000) exceeds the cumulative diluted EPS, making it antidilutive and excluded. Diluted EPS: $1,191,000 / 317,000 = $3.76. Choice D is incorrect because one antidilutive security does not require excluding all securities.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Corp. has 200,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and net income of $800,000. Redstone has written put options obligating it to repurchase 10,000 shares at $50 per share when the average market price is $40 per share. Under the reverse treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to the denominator for diluted EPS?', '["10,000 shares", "12,500 shares", "2,500 shares", "0 shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options use the reverse treasury stock method. The entity assumes it repurchases 10,000 shares at the $50 exercise price ($500,000 outflow) and issues shares at the $40 market price to fund the repurchase: $500,000 / $40 = 12,500 shares issued. Net incremental shares: 12,500 − 10,000 = 2,500. Choice A incorrectly uses the full number of shares under the put without applying the assumed issuance and repurchase mechanism.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Convertible bonds issued mid-year partial-year if-converted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, Weatherstone Inc. issues $600,000 of 6% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond convertible into 15 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Weatherstone has 100,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $500,000 for the full year. What is the after-tax interest add-back and the incremental shares for the convertible bonds when computing diluted EPS?', '["Interest add-back of $27,000 and 9,000 incremental shares", "Interest add-back of $13,500 and 4,500 incremental shares", "Interest add-back of $18,000 and 9,000 incremental shares", "Interest add-back of $13,500 and 9,000 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible bonds issued mid-year are included in diluted EPS from the issuance date. Interest for six months: $600,000 × 6% × 6/12 = $18,000, with an after-tax add-back of $13,500 ($18,000 × 0.75). Incremental shares are also time-weighted: 9,000 potential shares × 6/12 = 4,500. Choice D incorrectly includes 9,000 shares for the full year while correctly computing the interest adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — RSUs and share-based compensation in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Birchwood Corp. has 150,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $525,000. During the year, Birchwood granted employees 12,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) that vest over three years. As of year-end, $80,000 of total compensation cost remains unrecognized. The average market price of Birchwood''s stock is $40 per share. Under the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares from the RSUs are included in diluted EPS?', '["12,000 shares", "2,000 shares", "0 shares", "10,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, unvested share-based awards are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds equal the unrecognized compensation cost ($80,000), used to repurchase 2,000 shares ($80,000 / $40). The 12,000 RSUs minus 2,000 repurchased shares yield 10,000 incremental shares. Choice A incorrectly includes all 12,000 RSUs without accounting for the assumed share repurchase from unrecognized compensation cost.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — EPS presentation for discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartwell Corp. reports income from continuing operations of $720,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $120,000, for net income of $600,000. Hartwell has 200,000 weighted-average common shares, no preferred stock, and dilutive stock options that add 10,000 incremental shares. How should EPS be presented for each component?', '["Basic and diluted EPS must be presented for continuing operations, discontinued operations, and net income, using the same diluted denominator of 210,000 for all diluted amounts", "Basic and diluted EPS are required only for net income; continuing and discontinued per-share amounts are optional disclosures", "Diluted EPS should exclude the incremental shares from the discontinued operations line because that line is a loss", "Basic and diluted EPS are presented for continuing operations only; discontinued operations is shown net of tax without per-share data"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, an entity must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income. When discontinued operations exist, per-share amounts for that component are also required. The same diluted denominator (210,000) applies to all line items because the control number (continuing operations) is positive. Choice C is incorrect because the denominator is determined once by the control number, not recalculated per component.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Contracts settleable in stock or cash
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornfield Technologies has a performance bonus plan that allows the company to settle bonuses in either cash or common stock at management''s discretion. The potential bonus settlement could require issuing 8,000 shares. Net income is $500,000 with 100,000 weighted-average shares. How should these contracts affect diluted EPS?', '["The 8,000 shares are included in diluted EPS only if the company has historically settled in stock", "The 8,000 shares are excluded from diluted EPS because management can choose cash settlement", "The more dilutive of the two settlement methods is used, meaning the shares are presumed issued if stock settlement is more dilutive", "The 8,000 shares are always included in diluted EPS at one-half the total to reflect the dual settlement option"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-45, when a contract can be settled in either stock or cash, the entity must use the more dilutive assumption for computing diluted EPS. If stock settlement produces a more dilutive result, the potential shares are included in the denominator. Choice B is incorrect because management''s ability to choose cash settlement does not override the requirement to assume the more dilutive alternative in diluted EPS.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Weighted-average with stock dividend and new share issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Greystone Corp. has 80,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On March 1, Greystone declares a 25% stock dividend. On October 1, Greystone issues 10,000 new shares for cash. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for the year?', '["100,000 shares", "102,500 shares", "105,000 shares", "110,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are applied retroactively to January 1, increasing shares from 80,000 to 100,000 for the entire year. The 10,000 shares issued October 1 are time-weighted for three months: 10,000 × 3/12 = 2,500. Total weighted average is 102,500. Choice D (110,000) incorrectly treats the October 1 share issuance as outstanding for the full year rather than time-weighting it from the issuance date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred stock impact on EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pemberton Corp. issues 10,000 shares of Series C preferred stock at $50 par value with a mandatory redemption date in five years. The preferred stock pays a 6% annual dividend. Under ASC 480, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability. When computing basic EPS, the CFO subtracts the $30,000 preferred dividend from net income in the numerator. Evaluate whether this treatment is appropriate.', '["The CFO''s approach is correct; preferred dividends are always deducted from the EPS numerator regardless of classification", "The treatment is incorrect; the preferred stock should be reclassified as equity for EPS purposes and the dividends should be ignored entirely", "The treatment is incorrect; mandatorily redeemable preferred dividends should be added to the numerator to offset the interest charge", "The treatment is incorrect; because the preferred is classified as a liability under ASC 480, the payments are already reflected as interest expense in net income and should not be deducted again"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, and its dividend payments are reported as interest expense in arriving at net income. Because the amounts already reduce net income, deducting them again in the EPS numerator double-counts the charge. Choice A is incorrect because the EPS numerator adjustment for preferred dividends applies only to equity-classified preferred stock, not to liability-classified instruments under ASC 480.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Contingently issuable shares when condition not met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonebridge Holdings agreed to issue 50,000 additional common shares to the former owners of an acquired business if cumulative revenues from the acquired unit exceed $5 million within three years. At the end of Year 1, cumulative revenues are $1.2 million. Stonebridge has 300,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $900,000. Evaluate whether these shares should be included in basic and diluted EPS.', '["The shares are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because the revenue condition is not satisfied at period-end and would not be met if the period-end were the end of the contingency period", "The shares are excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS because all potentially issuable shares must be included in diluted calculations", "The shares are included in both basic and diluted EPS on a proportional basis: $1.2M / $5M × 50,000 = 12,000 shares", "The shares are included in diluted EPS at the full 50,000 because diluted EPS must assume all contingencies will be resolved favorably"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only if all conditions are met by period-end and in diluted EPS only if the conditions would be satisfied assuming the period-end were the end of the contingency period. With $1.2 million against a $5 million target, neither test is met. Choice B is incorrect because diluted EPS does not automatically include all potentially issuable shares; the period-end contingency test must be applied.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Rights issue with below-market bonus element
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what type of stock issuance requires an adjustment factor similar to a stock dividend when computing weighted-average shares?', '["A private placement at market price", "A secondary offering at market price", "A rights issue at below-market price", "A share repurchase in the open market"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-20, a rights issue offered to existing shareholders at below-market price contains a bonus element similar to a stock dividend. The bonus element requires an adjustment factor applied to the weighted-average shares for all periods before the rights issue. Choice A is incorrect because a private placement at market price does not contain a bonus element and requires no such adjustment.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Prior-period EPS retroactive restatement for stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Corp. reported basic EPS of $4.80 for the year ended December 31, 2024. In March 2025, Northgate declares a 3-for-1 stock split. When presenting comparative financial statements for years 2024 and 2025, how should the 2024 basic EPS be presented?', '["$4.80 with a footnote describing the subsequent stock split", "$1.60, retroactively adjusted for the 3-for-1 stock split", "$4.80 unadjusted, because prior-period EPS should not be restated for stock structure changes", "$2.40, adjusted using a 2-for-1 ratio because the split occurred in a subsequent year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits must be retroactively reflected in the weighted-average shares for all periods presented in comparative financial statements. Northgate''s 2024 basic EPS of $4.80 must be restated as $1.60 ($4.80 / 3) to reflect the 3-for-1 split. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly requires retroactive adjustment of prior-period EPS for stock splits and stock dividends.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L2 — Basic EPS with mid-year treasury stock repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries has 120,000 shares outstanding on January 1 and net income of $480,000. On April 1, Oakmont repurchases 20,000 shares as treasury stock. There are no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$4.00", "$4.80", "$4.36", "$4.57"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average shares must reflect the April 1 treasury stock repurchase from that date forward. Oakmont''s weighted average is 105,000 shares (120,000 × 3/12 + 100,000 × 9/12), producing basic EPS of $4.57 ($480,000 / 105,000). Choice C ($4.36) incorrectly uses a simple average of beginning and ending shares rather than properly time-weighting the repurchase.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Warrants diluted EPS using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakeview Corp. has 250,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and net income of $1,000,000. Lakeview has 15,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $30 per share. The average market price during the year is $50 per share. Using the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.91", "$3.77", "$4.00", "$3.85"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrant holders exercise at the $30 price, generating $450,000 in proceeds used to repurchase 9,000 shares at the $50 average market price. The 6,000 incremental shares (15,000 − 9,000) are added to the denominator: $1,000,000 / 256,000 = $3.91. Choice B ($3.77) incorrectly adds all 15,000 warrant shares without subtracting the shares assumed repurchased.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — At-the-money options dilutive effect by quarter
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Valleyfield Corp. has 10,000 stock options with an exercise price of $35. During the first quarter, the average market price is $35 per share. During the second quarter, the average market price rises to $45 per share. Using the treasury stock method, what is the dilutive effect of these options in each respective quarter?', '["The options are dilutive in both quarters because they are outstanding throughout", "The options add 2,222 incremental shares in the first quarter and zero in the second quarter", "The options add zero incremental shares in the first quarter and 2,222 incremental shares in the second quarter", "The options are antidilutive in both quarters because the exercise price equals or exceeds the market price in at least one period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, options are dilutive only when the average market price exceeds the exercise price. In Q1, the $35 exercise price equals the $35 market price, producing zero incremental shares (10,000 − 10,000 = 0). In Q2, proceeds of $350,000 repurchase only 7,778 shares at $45, yielding 2,222 incremental shares. Choice B reverses the quarters, incorrectly treating Q1 as dilutive and Q2 as non-dilutive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Partially paid shares in weighted-average calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fernbrook Corp. has 100,000 fully paid common shares outstanding. On May 1, Fernbrook issues 20,000 partially paid shares for which subscribers have paid 60% of the $25 per share issue price. The remaining 40% is collected on November 1. Evaluate how these partially paid shares should be included in the weighted-average share calculation for the year and determine the total weighted-average shares.', '["113,333 shares; the 20,000 shares are included at full value from May 1 regardless of payment status", "109,333 shares; the partially paid shares are included proportional to the amount paid, with 12,000 equivalent shares for six months and 20,000 for two months", "100,000 shares; partially paid shares are excluded entirely until fully paid", "108,000 shares; only the 12,000 equivalent shares at 60% paid are used for all eight months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, partially paid shares are included in the weighted-average proportional to the amount paid. From May through October, 20,000 × 60% = 12,000 equivalent shares for 6 months. From November through December, the fully paid 20,000 shares for 2 months. Weighted contribution: (12,000 × 6/12) + (20,000 × 2/12) = 9,333, yielding total weighted-average of 109,333. Choice A incorrectly includes all shares at full value from the issuance date.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Year-to-date interim period EPS computation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearfield Corp. has 200,000 shares outstanding on January 1 and issues 60,000 additional shares on May 1. Net income for the six-month interim period ended June 30 is $506,000, with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS for the interim period?', '["$2.30", "$2.20", "$1.95", "$2.53"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, year-to-date EPS for interim periods uses the weighted-average shares computed over the entire year-to-date period. Clearfield''s weighted average is 220,000 shares (200,000 × 4/6 + 260,000 × 2/6), yielding basic EPS of $2.30 ($506,000 / 220,000). Choice B ($2.20) incorrectly uses a simple average of beginning and ending shares (230,000) rather than a time-weighted average.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Definition of if-converted method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does the if-converted method assume when computing diluted EPS?', '["Convertible securities are valued at their fair market price on the last day of the period", "Convertible securities are tested for impairment before inclusion in the EPS computation", "Convertible securities generate taxable income upon conversion that must be included in the numerator", "Convertible securities were converted into common stock at the beginning of the period or at issuance, whichever is later"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes that convertible bonds or convertible preferred stock were converted into common shares at the beginning of the reporting period or the issuance date if later. The numerator is adjusted for interest or dividends avoided, and the denominator is increased by the potential shares. Choice A is incorrect because the conversion rate is contractually fixed, not based on period-end market price.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
